nning uh it  was mostly a technical skill  we're generating this insight and we're  happy about it it was mostly computer  scientists playing around but then tim  came along and he said look  there  you're missing something there is this  human  who who should receive these  explanations and  you you're basically generating it for  the human and not just in a vacuum  and then the field kind of shifted  completely  more towards humans so how do humans  perceive explanations what sort of  explanations humans like  they should be interactive probably  conversational  probably counterfactuals so we really  like hypothetical situations what if  well if this was not as it is how would  it end up  but then i think that  we got so  in love with this concept  that we actually started  overcompensating and focusing too much  on humans and treating these systems  only from a human perspective whereas  if you think about it carefully  explainers  are technical objects so they may  generate these insights that are  directed towards humans but they also  have a lot of technical things going  underneath the hood  so  let me give you two examples one is from  a technical perspective and the other  one is from more from a social  perspective we'll start with the  technical bit  so  let's assume that we have an image  and some predictive model and this is  what we get out of that model so it's  mostly 99  tennis ball prediction and then some  probability for  a golden retriever and labrador  retriever  and we're asking why  why is this particular  prediction  reasonable  so this is an example of an explanation  and let me walk you through that so  what's happening here is we take this  image  we split it into non-overlapping chunks  and  we probe the model by saying well  if we remove one of these chunks or a  random combination of these chunks how  does the model respond  so we're basically trying to see  which parts of this image are sensitive  for a particular prediction  and then once we run the my stats so we  we  remove random patterns based on this  division of the image and we quantify  how much does removing certain patterns  certain parts of this image influence  the prediction and this is our  explanation so the two pictures on the  left  are explanations  visualized in a different way so  that they are complementary actually so  it tells you well segment number one  and number five which are the two in the  background  contribute negatively towards the  prediction of a tennis ball and then the  ones colored in green contribute in a  positive way towards that prediction  so having these segments there  gives you a tangible predictions whereas  if you remove them you may not get  a tennis ball fraction and then the  the bar chart quantifies how much  this actually these segments actually  influence the prediction  and then we can do and you know this is  kind of reasonable there is some  background noise there because you get  these important bits of the background  the image which you may question further  but in essence it picks up on the tennis  ball which is great  but what if you explain golden retriever  well then you get  some positive influence from the  background and what this explanation  basically tells you well if the tennis  ball is not there then it's a golden  retriever which isn't  a very good explanation  so  you may want to probe that further  and  this is what i've got here so basically  there are two  parameters for this explainer it's a  very basic explainer  and what you can change is one how you  remove information from that image  because you obviously can't just  remove them you need a proxy for that  and the proxy in this case is occlusion  so you remove information from the image  by replacing that particular partition  of the image with a fixed color so in  this case we have a bunch of colors to  test  and i'll go through them in a moment and  then the second parameter is  how many segments you generate so how do  you split  the image so you can increase  the number of segments and on the right  here you can see the randomization  pattern that you get so these are all of  these images that then  we use to probe the  black box  and then the explanation that we get on  the left  so  if you look carefully at that if we  change the occlusion color  the explanation changes quite  drastically  and this is also  relevant for other colors  so you can see that this parameter  actually  affects quite significantly how we  generate the explanation what we get out  of it  and then  the same applies to  how many segments we generate for that  image  so if we increase the number of segments  you can again see that the explanation  changes  and  as you increase the number of segments  it changes even further an interesting  observation here  is that actually some  some parameter choices are more  reasonable than others  and if we look at  this occlusion  which basically replaces  each part of the image with the average  color  in that segment  as we increase  the number of segments  you can see that the removal strategy  isn't quite that effective what we're  basically doing we're blurring the image  we're decreasing its quality but we're  not really hiding the information  and this is the core premise of this  explainer we want to remove this  information in order to quantify  how much it influences the prediction so  if we go ahead with this method and use  the average occlusion and then split the  image into multiple segments we're  basically not doing anything we're just  so so then the explanation won't be  meaningful possibly  so that's one aspect  of explainability the technical aspect  and then there's the other one which is  how do you present this information so  you've seen with the previous one there  were two complementary figures one was  the importance overlaid on top of the  image  and the other one was a bar plot that  showed the  magnitude  of these importance factors  but there are multiple ways so  let's say that we have distilled our  explanation into this linear equation  and this may not be very informative for  you and you may just doze off now saying  okay that's  i don't want to have to deal with that  but it actually  so we there are a few ways in which you  can interpret this so one is you forget  about your data points so this your data  point this is the  the explanation the model that is  reasonably simple  so you may just focus on the top part  and i say okay the feature number one  is important 0.25 this is the importance  then feature number four is 0.7 but then  if you want to apply it to a specific  instance specific data point you  actually need to do some math  and then you plug this vector in  you do calculations and this is what you  get as a result so then you can kind of  quantify  the importance of each  feature for this particular instance  but that again requires quite a lot of  reasoning and you need to know what  you're doing so we probably can simplify  that  and that's basically  more or less the same information we  lose quite a lot because we don't get  the equation we can't experiment with it  anymore but this is  representing the same information  for the particular instance that i  showed you on the previous slide so  that's a bit more reasonable you see  that the base prediction is  0.2 and then feature number one pushes  it a bit to the right  and then five and seven to the left and  number four  more to do all right and you end up with  prediction 0.6 so more or less it  captures the same information and  probably that's more accessible  but we can go in further so here again  we required some reasoning  and that's again the same information  shown in a completely different way  and i would argue that this is  yet even more accessible so you see that  there is this base value of 0.2 you end  up with a prediction of 0.6 and then  there are these features that push to  the left and to the right  and you know you end up here  so  there's less reasoning required on the  human part to actually get what's  happening  so  having seen that there are these two  aspects the social one  and the technical one can we actually  use this to map out  how explainability works  and this are a few of my ideas so first  of all just asking  does the explanation work is meaningless  how do you define work is it your user  study if the users were happy with it  then it works but there is so much  context that you can't capture  that i think it's meaningless to ask  these sort of questions so  can we zoom in  and i think that by distinguishing  between the technical aspects  and the presentation aspects the social  aspect we can actually do a bit better  so  then we have  two ideas to navigate one  is the explanatory insight correct and  by explanatory insight i mean the  numerical representation that you  extract as your explanation  is it correct or is it not  and then the other aspect is how do you  present them is it convincing is it  accessible or not and then you may end  up with all sorts of different troubles  so for example  if your insight is correct  but the the presentation medium isn't  very good you're not really explaining  because  you won't communicate effectively  if you are presenting it in a convincing  and effective way and it is correct then  great that's basically explainability  but then you can go all the way to the  left side so  if it's incorrect but convincing  then you're basically persuading people  you're saying look this is what it is  and it's potentially harmful if people  believe in that  then  well if you want to manipulate people  that's great but if you want to explain  it's not so good  and then if it's incorrect and  ineffective well you're not doing much  but that's just capturing one aspect of  it so here we're dealing with the model  and with the inside but if we go back  to the actual phenomenon that generates  the data then we are in a completely  different  we're asking a completely different  question because now we're saying well  if the model is correct with respect to  the phenomenon  and we're generating some insights these  are  explanations because they correspond to  how the world works  whereas if the model is incorrect if it  predicts wrong  and you're generating some insights then  what you're doing is basically debugging  the model you're trying to figure out  why the model  gave you the wrong answer so that's not  so much explainability  as debugging so it would serve a  different role basically  and  having said that  what what can we learn from this sort of  differentiation  well first of all  you probably need more than one insight  and possibly spanning more than one  category of explanations so you want to  see  many insights and then reason about them  to  build a coherent picture  of what's happening inside your  predictive model  then also explainability is not really a  binary thing because what is explainable  to you may not be explainable to me what  i may find  transparent for other people maybe  you'll pay so for example if you go back  to the example of different presentation  modes then i can handle the equation  just fine whereas other people may  prefer  one of the plots and  you know there are different  different ways in which we understand  so basically  this would lead us to a conclusion that  explainability tools  are just diagnostic tools there is not  much explanation happening unless we  actually understand  what are the assumptions under which we  operate  and how to interpret their results  because you may see a bar plot  and i may see a bar plot and we may have  a completely different perception of  what it conveys  just because i may have some background  if you don't  and this actually leads me to finally  define explainability  so i define explainability as  understanding it has to lead to  something  and whatever understanding is in this  case i don't know it was probably  context dependent but what you want in  the end is understanding  and understanding is achieved by  gathering some transparent insight and  again transpiring this subjective  you want something that is transparent  to you  and you reason about it given your  background  knowledge whether it's technical  knowledge  or  domain knowledge and you could see that  for example  with the the very first example that i  gave with a linear equation you actually  had to do some reasoning in order to  extract the explanation possibly  generate some meanings and understanding  and this very much depends on what you  present  in what context and to whom  thanks so much  [Applause]  foreign

